## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic, per-tenant tracing in a multi-DC docker-dca setup, including config retrieval, TTL-based caching, and graceful degradation when the sidecar cannot fetch config.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry sampling and per-tenant configuration
- Consul KV as a central per-tenant policy store
- TTL caching to limit external calls
- Graceful fallback to sensible default rate
- Cross-DC deployment considerations and observability impact

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-sampler for per-tenant tracing
class TenantSampler {
  constructor(fetchRate) {
    this.fetchRate = fetchRate; // function(tenant) -> rate
    this.cache = new Map(); // tenant -> {rate, expiry}
  }
  getRate(tenant) {
    const entry = this.cache.get(tenant);
    if (entry && entry.expiry > Date.now()) return entry.rate;
    // fetch from Consul KV here, then cache for 5s
    const rate = this.fetchRate(tenant) ?? 20;
    this.cache.set(tenant, { rate, expiry: Date.now() + 5000 });
    return rate;
  }
  shouldSample(tenant) {
    const rate = this.getRate(tenant);
    return Math.random() * 100 < rate;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness of per-tenant rates during rollout?
- What are failure modes if Consul KV is unavailable, and how to mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in edge proxying, per-tenant isolation, simple sidecar patterns, and in-memory caching with TTL. It also exercises docker-compose bootstrapping, TLS termination, and cache invalidation on 5xx.

## Key Concepts
- In-memory TTL cache and eviction
- Per-tenant cache keys and header reliance
- Sidecar design that intercepts requests
- TLS termination at an edge proxy and routing by tenant
- Cache invalidation on error responses

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple in-memory TTL cache for GET responses
const cache = new Map();
function cacheMiddleware(req, res, next) {
  if (req.method !== 'GET') return next();
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'] || 'default';
  const key = `${tenant}|${req.originalUrl}`;
  const now = Date.now();
  const entry = cache.get(key);
  if (entry && now < entry.expires) {
    res.set(entry.headers);
    return res.end(entry.body);
  }
  const origSend = res.send.bind(res);
  res.send = (body) => {
    cache.set(key, { body, expires: now + 60000, headers: res.getHeaders() });
    return origSend(body);
  };
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this cache across multiple instances?
- How would you protect against unbounded memory growth?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests automation, Vault + SPIFFE integration, and zero-downtime trust reconfiguration across DCs.

## Key Concepts
- Vault CA bundles and SPIFFE trust anchors
- SPIFFE SVID rotation and edge-proxy hot-reload
- Idempotence, rollback, and verification across DCs

## Code Example
```javascript
// bootstrap trust distribution (pseudocode)
async function bootstrapCrossDC(dc) {
  await fetchTrustAnchors(dc);
  await rotateSVIDs(dc);
  await hotReloadProxies(dc);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift between trust material and service SVIDs?
- How do you handle partial failures during bootstrap (e.g., one DC unreachable)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests edge routing with simple feature flag logic using standard nginx capabilities, keeping it beginner-friendly while still requiring practical setup.

## Key Concepts
- nginx upstreams and map for header-driven routing
- TLS termination in docker-compose
- cross-DC networking and service naming

## Code Example
```nginx
http {
  upstream api-old { server api-old:8080; }
  upstream api-new { server api-new:8080; }
  map $http_x_feature_flag_alpha $target { default api-old; 1 api-new; }
  server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    ssl_certificate /certs/server.crt; ssl_certificate_key /certs/server.key;
    location / { proxy_pass http://$target; }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test header-driven routing in CI?
- How would you add health checks and a safe rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical knowledge of per-tenant configuration via Docker Config, feature gating, and edge proxy routing in a multi-DC setup.

## Key Concepts
- Docker Config for per-tenant rules
- Feature flag gating in API
- Traefik TLS and docker-dca wiring
- Lightweight bootstrap and config reload

## Code Example
```javascript
// sample Express middleware for feature gate
const fs = require('fs');
let flags = {};
try { flags = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('/config/feature-flags.json','utf8')); } catch(e) { flags = {}; }
function featureGate(req,res,next){
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant'];
  if(!tenant) return res.status(400).send('Missing X-Tenant');
  const enabled = flags[tenant] && flags[tenant].includes('new-feature');
  if(!enabled) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
  next();
}
app.get('/new-feature', featureGate, (req,res)=>{ res.send('New feature access granted'); });
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you hot-reload the flags without restarting the API?
- How would you test isolation across tenants when flags change?
